<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Ney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Marshal Ney</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Ney was a French soldier and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s marshals, created Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elchingen">Elchingen</a> in 1808 for his victory there in 1805 and later Prince of the Moskva for his part at Borodino. Famed for his courage in commanding the rearguard on the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1812, he rallied to Napoleon during the Hundred Days and led the cavalry charges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. He was tried for treason and shot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in December 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-ney.jpg" alt="Michel Ney" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marechal_Ney.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michel Ney was born on 10 January 1769 in Sarrelouis, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> on the French-German border, the second son of a master cooper, Pierre Ney, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. The town was a French enclave in a predominantly German region and Ney grew up bilingual. After schooling until 1782 he worked as a notary's clerk and then in mines and forges. Civil life did not suit him, and in 1787 he enlisted in the Colonel-General Hussar Regiment. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> the officer corps was closed to those without noble ancestry, but he rose quickly through the non-commissioned ranks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ney fought at Valmy in 1792 and was commissioned that October. He was wounded at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1793, and as a cavalry commander was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> on 17 April 1797 and exchanged on 8 May. He became a general of division in March 1799 and fought at Hohenlinden in December 1800. On 19 May 1804 he received his marshal's baton. He commanded VI Corps in 1805, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Eylau, and was made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elchingen">Elchingen</a> on 6 June 1808. In 1810 he invaded Portugal with Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">André Masséna</a>, and after bitter complaints about the campaign he was removed for insubordination.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 Ney led III Corps, was wounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, fought at Borodino and commanded the rearguard on the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Cut off at Krasnoi, he escaped across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> over fragile ice with heavy losses and rejoined the army at Orsha. Napoleon called him the bravest of the brave, and he was made Prince de la Moskowa on 25 March 1813. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> on 4 April 1814 he spoke for the marshals demanding Napoleon's abdication, then served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>, who made him a Peer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> returned, Ney set out to stop him but joined him at Lons-le-Saulnier on 14 March 1815 with about 6,000 men. At Quatre Bras on 16 June he was criticised for attacking slowly, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> on 18 June he ordered a mass cavalry charge that failed to break the infantry squares; five horses were killed under him. Arrested on 3 August, he was tried before the Chamber of Peers after the court-martial declared itself not competent. The Peers voted 137 for death, and he was shot near the Luxembourg Gardens on 7 December 1815. Accounts of his last words differ. He is buried at Père Lachaise Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michel Ney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Michel Ney</b>, whom Napoleon called 'the bravest of the brave', was shot in Paris for treason after joining his former emperor during the Hundred Days.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 14 October 1805 <b>Marshal Ney</b> defeated an Austrian force at the Battle of Elchingen during the Ulm campaign and was later made Duke of Elchingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-elchingen">Imperial Abbey of Elchingen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saarlouis, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.32,6.75&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Saarlouis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4706 444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michel Ney"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.32,6.75&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
